The issue is that `typec_partner_link_device` may fail to create the "typec" symlink (or the link never gets created if the partner registers after the USB device attaches), yet `port->usb2_dev` and `port->usb3_dev` still get set. Later, when `typec_unregister_partner` runs, it tries to unlink these devices even though the "typec" link was never established in the first place, causing `sysfs_remove_link` to fail on a nonexistent link and triggering a kernel warning about ENOENT in `kernfs_remove_by_name_ns`.

Looking more closely at `typec_partner_attach`, the USB device pointers are assigned even when there's no partner yet, so `link_device` never gets called. Then when the partner is eventually registered, `typec_register_partner` attempts to create the link if those USB device pointers are already set. I need to check what happens in that function to understand the full flow.
